    "_meanings": [
        "One another, each other, mutual (treated like a pronoun). In many cases\n the use of this word corresponds to the use of the word ‘each other’ or\n ‘one another’ in <lang>English</lang>; {#anyonyaM tAqayataH#} <ls>Mk. 9</ls> they strike each other\n\n ({#anyaH anyaM tAqayati#}). Thus {#anyaH#} may be regarded as the subject\n and {#anyam#} as the object of the verb, as in <lang>English</lang>. The second {#anya#}\n may, therefore, in many cases stand in the <ab>instr.</ab>, <ab>gen.</ab>, or <ab>loc.</ab> cases;\n {#anyonyErAhatAH santaH sasvanurBImaniHsvanAH#} <ls>Rām.</ls>; {#anyonyasya vyatilunanti#}\n <ls>P. I. 3. 16. Sk.</ls> But there are several instances, especially when {#anyonya#}\n enters into compound, in which the first {#anya#} loses all its nominative\n force and becomes a sort of oblique case, or an irregular compound of\n {#anya#} and {#anya#}, see <ls>P. VIII. 1. 12. Sk.</ls>; {#anyonyasyAvyaBIcAraH#}\n <ls>Ms. 9. 101</ls>; <ab>oft.</ab> in <ab>comp.</ab> and translated by ‘mutual’, ‘reciprocal’, ‘mutually’;\n {#˚SoBAjananAt#} <ls>Ku. 1. 42</ls>; so {#˚kalaha, ˚darSana#}, <ab>&c.</ab>\n"
    ],
